                           THE DRILL HALL



   Yari came through the north doors with her bag and went straight to
the nearest person who looked unsteady, which was the old woman
with the long horn, and put two fingers on her wrist.
   “I’m fine,” said the old woman. “I’m eighty-one.”
   “Then you’ll sit,” said Yari, and she did.
   Rafa had stepped off the seam in the floor, finally, and was standing
in the middle of the white carpet with his palo on his shoulder. He
wasn’t playing it. He was looking at the roof with his head tilted, the
way you listen to a thing breathing in another room.
   Then he looked at me and nodded once.
   It’s open.
   I sat down on the trolley.
   It was the first time in an hour I’d stopped moving. My legs ached.
My head was ringing. I put my hand on the case and felt La Madre
through the cloth, still warm, and I let out a breath I’d been holding
since March.
   “JC,” said Yanaisa.
   “We did it,” I said. “We did it. It’s open. It’s—”
   “JC.”
   She had hold of my wrist. Her eyes had gone very wide. Yúcahu, in
the sling against her chest, was glowing faintly through the fabric.
   “It’s not over,” she said.



On the stage, Vale had stopped trying to speak.
   He stood at the lectern with his hands on its edges, looking at the
room. I watched him take it in: the chairs, the horns, the vest, the cam‐
eras. He looked like a man looking at a ledger that has stopped adding
up.
   The venue manager and two guards reached him at the foot of the
stage. They were asking about the curtain. They were asking about the
latch. They were asking about a man in a gray polo with a bruised fore‐
arm who’d been seen laying hands on a guest.
   He turned toward them with perfect courtesy.




